  "body": {
    "id": "prop:first-lossy-size",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "Among all admissible positive even population sizes not exceeding eight, the equality set is exactly \\(\\{2,4,6\\}\\), and \\(n=8\\) is the first size at which compressing \\((X_1,X_0)\\) to \\(W\\) is minimax-lossy for ATE squared error.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "n",
      "X_1",
      "X_0",
      "W"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "thm:n24-equality",
      "thm:n6-seven-state-saddle",
      "thm:n8-strict-separation"
    ],
    "justification": "This is the finite threshold result that turns four isolated calculations into one interpretable theorem.",
    "gap": "The closest papers leave unrestricted complete-randomization behavior unresolved and do not identify a first sample-size cutoff for count compression.",
    "consumer": "DeclareDesign users receive the first exact sample size at which the conventional count difference ceases to be a minimax-preserving data reduction.",
    "proof_tex": "By definition, admissible population sizes are positive even integers.  The complete list not exceeding eight is therefore \\(2,4,6,8\\).  Theorem \\(\\mathrm{thm{:}n24\\mbox{-}equality}\\) proves \\(\\rho_n^{\\mathrm{CR}}=\\rho_n^W\\) for \\(n=2,4\\), Theorem \\(\\mathrm{thm{:}n6\\mbox{-}seven\\mbox{-}state\\mbox{-}saddle}\\) proves equality for \\(n=6\\), and Theorem \\(\\mathrm{thm{:}n8\\mbox{-}strict\\mbox{-}separation}\\) proves \\(\\rho_8^{\\mathrm{CR}}<\\rho_8^W\\).  Hence the equality set among admissible sizes at most eight is exactly \\(\\{2,4,6\\}\\), and eight is the first size at which the compression to \\(W\\) is minimax-lossy.",
